    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently reviewing the cataloging strategy for a new acquisition of 19th-century manuscripts here at our university archives. We are trying to decide whether to stick with the Dewey Decimal System or transition these specific collections to the Library of Congress Classification. Could you provide a comparative analysis of these two systems in terms of their metadata granularity and scalability for specialized historical archives?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dewey Decimal System (DDC) offers a straightforward, hierarchical structure, which can be useful for broad, general-audience cataloging. However, for specialized 19th-century manuscripts, it often lacks the necessary fine-grained control. The Library of Congress Classification (LCC), on the other hand, provides a much deeper, more nuanced classification scheme, which is better suited for the complex, specialized nature of historical archives. For scalability, LCC generally handles the vast and varied subject matter of historical collections with greater precision.",
