    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One of my main concerns with the Dewey approach is that because it's so broad, we might end up with a lot of \"miscellaneous\" or unclassified bins for items that don't fit perfectly. It feels like things could easily get lost or messy in those catch-all categories. In your experience, how do these two systems handle large volumes of \"overflow\" or items that escape standard categorization?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "DDC's tendency toward broad categories is exactly where the \"miscellaneous\" bin problem arises, which is a real risk for valuable historical material. In LCC, while overflow still exists, its structure allows for more specific shelving guides and subject headings that can capture the *flavor* of an item even if it doesn't fit neatly into a primary class. LCC's flexibility, through its detailed subdivisions, helps prevent items from being lost in a generic \"catch-all\" pile, making it much more robust for complex, idiosyncratic manuscripts.",
      "source": "target",
